`CollapseLeadingSlashes` only acts when `url[0] == '/'`. A target such as `\\evil.com` passes through unchanged. Line 48 then sees `pattern[0] != '/'` and prepends '/', producing `/\\evil.com`. Browsers treat that as a scheme-relative authority, so the open redirect this change is meant to close is still reachable. One example is a rule with `url="{R:1}"` and a request path like `legacy/%5Cevil.com`.

Suggestion: Prepend the '/' first and collapse afterwards:

if (!pattern.Contains(Uri.SchemeDelimiter, StringComparison.Ordinal) && pattern[0] != '/')
{
    pattern = '/' + pattern;
}
pattern = UrlNormalizer.CollapseLeadingSlashes(pattern);

