`CollapseLeadingSlashes` only acts when `pattern[0] == '/'`. A pattern that starts with '\' passes through unchanged. The next statement then prepends '/', producing `/\example.com`. Browsers treat that as the scheme-relative `//example.com`.

IIS match URLs are evaluated without the leading slash. So a rule such as `<action type="Redirect" url="{R:0}" />` (or any target that starts with a back-reference) and a request to `/\evil.com` yields `Location: /\evil.com`. The open redirect remains. The new tests only cover inputs that already begin with '/', so they miss this case.

Suggestion: Prepend '/' first and normalize afterwards:

if (!pattern.Contains(Uri.SchemeDelimiter, StringComparison.Ordinal) && pattern[0] != '/')
{
    pattern = '/' + pattern;
}
pattern = UrlNormalizer.CollapseLeadingSlashes(pattern);

Alternatively, make CollapseLeadingSlashes treat a leading '\' the same as '/'. Add a test case with the request path `/\example.com` and the target `{R:0}`.

The check runs before `RedirectAction` and `RedirectRule` add a `/` to relative targets, but it only handles values that already start with `/`. A target such as `\example.com` or `\/example.com` (for example, `$1` capturing a backslash-prefixed path) is skipped here and then becomes `/\example.com` once the `/` is added. Browsers treat that as a scheme-relative URL, so the open redirect is still possible. Collapse any leading run of `/` or `\`, including one that starts with a backslash.

Improved code:
public static string CollapseLeadingSlashes(string url)
{
    if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(url))
    {
        return url;
    }

    if (url[0] != '/' && url[0] != '\\')
    {
        return url;
    }

    if (url[0] == '/' && (url.Length < 2 || (url[1] != '/' && url[1] != '\\')))
    {
        return url;
    }

    var i = 0;
    while (i < url.Length && (url[i] == '/' || url[i] == '\\'))
    {
        i++;
    }

    return i == url.Length ? "/" : string.Concat("/", url.AsSpan(i));
}

The collapse runs before the `/` prefix is added, so a pattern starting with a backslash slips through. For example, a back-reference that yields `\evil.com` is left unchanged by `CollapseLeadingSlashes`, then gets prefixed to `/\evil.com`. Browsers treat `/\evil.com` as a scheme-relative authority, which is an open redirect. Normalize after the prefix so that every relative target is collapsed. The same ordering issue likely applies in `RedirectRule` and `RewriteAction`, where a `/` is also prepended after the new call.

