The early return requires url[0] == '/'. Browsers treat '\' like '/' in URLs, so `\\example.com` and `\/example.com` are still scheme-relative authorities. They pass through unchanged. This matters in RedirectRule, where newPath is written to the Location header with no further prefixing. The comment says it mirrors SharedUrlHelper.IsLocalUrl, but that predicate only needs to reject these inputs, not normalize them, so the claim does not make the function safe for them.

Suggestion: Treat both characters as slashes at index 0:

static bool IsSlash(char c) => c == '/' || c == '\\';
if (url.Length < 2 || !IsSlash(url[0]) || !IsSlash(url[1])) return url;
// then skip the whole leading run and return "/" + rest

