`_hasDeferredDynamicOverflowUpdate` is cleared only in `ApplyDeferredDynamicOverflowUpdate`. If opening the popup throws, that method is never reached, so the flag stays set. A stale update would then run on a later open, re-shuffling `_overflowItems` while the popup is realizing items. Clear the flag whenever a full update runs directly, since that update already covers any pending request.

Improved code:
private void RequestDynamicOverflowUpdate()
{
    if (_isOpeningOverflowPopup)
    {
        _hasDeferredDynamicOverflowUpdate = true;
        return;
    }

    _hasDeferredDynamicOverflowUpdate = false;
    UpdateDynamicOverflow();
}

ApplyDeferredDynamicOverflowUpdate() sits after the try/finally. If `_overflowPopup.IsOpen = true` throws, the finally resets `_isOpeningOverflowPopup` but the deferred update is never applied. `_hasDeferredDynamicOverflowUpdate` stays true, so the overflow and visible-primary collections can be stale for changes made during the failed open. The leftover flag also triggers an unrelated recalculation the next time the popup opens.

Suggestion: Apply the deferred update from the finally block, or clear `_hasDeferredDynamicOverflowUpdate` before setting IsOpen so a failed open cannot leave a stale flag. For example:

_hasDeferredDynamicOverflowUpdate = false;
try { _isOpeningOverflowPopup = true; _overflowPopup.IsOpen = true; }
finally { _isOpeningOverflowPopup = false; }
ApplyDeferredDynamicOverflowUpdate();

ApplyDeferredDynamicOverflowUpdate() sits after the try/finally. If `_overflowPopup.IsOpen = true` throws, the finally resets `_isOpeningOverflowPopup` but the deferred update is never applied. `_hasDeferredDynamicOverflowUpdate` stays true, so the overflow and visible-primary collections can be stale for changes made during the failed open. The leftover flag also triggers an unrelated recalculation the next time the popup opens.

Suggestion: Apply the deferred update from the finally block, or clear `_hasDeferredDynamicOverflowUpdate` before setting IsOpen so a failed open cannot leave a stale flag. For example:

_hasDeferredDynamicOverflowUpdate = false;
try { _isOpeningOverflowPopup = true; _overflowPopup.IsOpen = true; }
finally { _isOpeningOverflowPopup = false; }
ApplyDeferredDynamicOverflowUpdate();

`ApplyDeferredDynamicOverflowUpdate()` only runs when `_overflowPopup != null`. If the popup is null or the flag is set elsewhere, a deferred update can be left pending. More importantly, if `IsOpen` is set to false and re-opened, a stale flag could trigger an unexpected update. Resetting the flag in the `finally` block and applying the deferred update there ensures the update is not lost even if setting `IsOpen` throws.

Improved code:
if (_overflowPopup != null)
                    {
                        try
                        {
                            _isOpeningOverflowPopup = true;
                            _overflowPopup.IsOpen = true;
                        }
                        finally
                        {
                            _isOpeningOverflowPopup = false;
                            ApplyDeferredDynamicOverflowUpdate();
                        }
                    }

If `_overflowPopup.IsOpen = true` throws, `ApplyDeferredDynamicOverflowUpdate()` is skipped. `_hasDeferredDynamicOverflowUpdate` then stays set until the next update request, so the overflow layout can be left stale. Move the deferred-update call into the `finally` block so it runs on every exit path.

Improved code:
try
{
    _isOpeningOverflowPopup = true;
    _overflowPopup.IsOpen = true;
}
finally
{
    _isOpeningOverflowPopup = false;
    ApplyDeferredDynamicOverflowUpdate();
}

`host?.Height = 40;` is only valid with null-conditional assignment (C# 14). With older `LangVersion` settings it fails to compile. `host` is also effectively required for the test to be meaningful, so silently skipping on null hides failures.

Suggestion: var host = Assert.IsAssignableFrom<ContentControl>(flyout.Popup.Host);
host.Height = 40;

The new guard also discards repositions caused by `_childMargin` changes, not only by size changes. When the margin changes, such as when the shadow or decoration margin is updated, the popup is no longer shifted to compensate, so its content is visually offset from the pointer anchor. Suppress only the size-triggered reposition and keep margin-triggered updates.

Improved code:
if (_popupSize != size)
{
    _popupSize = size;
    if (!(_hasInitialPosition && _popupPositionRequest?.Placement == PlacementMode.Pointer))
    {
        reposition = true;
    }
}

`host?.Height = 40;` uses null-conditional assignment, which requires C# 14 and fails to compile with older language versions. If `host` is null, the test also silently skips the resize and passes vacuously. Assert that `host` is non-null and use plain member access.

Improved code:
Assert.NotNull(host);
host.Height = 40;
host.InvalidateMeasure();
host.Measure(Size.Infinity);
host.Arrange(new Rect(default, host.DesiredSize));

The new check sets `reposition = false` unconditionally for pointer-placed popups once positioned. That also suppresses repositioning triggered by other conditions earlier in `ArrangeSetBounds`, such as a `_childMargin` change. Only a size change should be ignored, so restrict the suppression to the size branch to avoid a behavioural regression.

Improved code:
if (_popupSize != size)
{
    _popupSize = size;

    // Pointer-placed popups keep their initial position when only their size changes.
    if (!(_hasInitialPosition && _popupPositionRequest?.Placement == PlacementMode.Pointer))
    {
        reposition = true;
    }
}

`host?.Height = 40;` uses null-conditional assignment, which only C# 14 supports. It will not compile with older language versions. If `host` is null, the test also silently skips the resize and passes without checking anything. Assert that `host` is non-null and use normal member access.

Improved code:
Assert.NotNull(host);
host.Height = 40;
host.InvalidateMeasure();
host.Measure(Size.Infinity);
host.Arrange(new Rect(default, host.DesiredSize));

`_hasInitialPosition` is never reset when the popup is reused (e.g. reopened with a new `_popupPositionRequest`) because it is only cleared in `Dispose()`. A popup that is shown again at a new pointer position would then never reposition, since `reposition` is forced to false. Reset the flag when a new position request is set (or when the popup is hidden) so each show gets its initial placement.

Improved code:
if (_hasInitialPosition && _popupPositionRequest?.Placement == PlacementMode.Pointer)
{
    reposition = false;
}

if (reposition)
{
    if (_popupPositionRequest != null)
    {
        _hasInitialPosition = true;
    }
// NOTE: also set `_hasInitialPosition = false;` in the method that assigns a new
// `_popupPositionRequest` (e.g. `ConfigurePosition`) and in the hide/close path.

Assigning through a null-conditional (`host?.Height = 40`) requires C# 14 and silently does nothing if `host` is null, which would let the test pass without exercising the resize. Assert that `host` is non-null and use plain assignments.

Improved code:
Assert.NotNull(host);
host.Height = 40;
host.InvalidateMeasure();
host.Measure(Size.Infinity);
host.Arrange(new Rect(default, host.DesiredSize));

`_hasInitialPosition` is only reset in `Dispose()`. If the same `PopupRoot` is hidden and shown again (or the position request changes, e.g. a new pointer location), the flag stays true and `Pointer` placement will never reposition, leaving the popup at a stale location. Reset the flag whenever the position request is updated or the popup is re-shown, and clear it before disposing the platform impl.

Improved code:
public void Dispose()
{
    _hasInitialPosition = false;
    PlatformImpl?.Dispose();
    EnsureClosed();
}

public void ConfigurePosition(PopupPositionRequest request)
{
    // A new position request must be allowed to reposition the popup at least once.
    _hasInitialPosition = false;
    _popupPositionRequest = request;
    _needsUpdate = true;
    UpdatePosition();
}

The flag is set as soon as a request exists, even if `UpdatePosition()` could not actually apply it (for example, an early arrange before the platform impl/target is ready). Later resizes are then suppressed and the popup never reaches the correct position. Only suppress repositioning when the position was actually applied, and don't mark it as initial when no request-based placement happened.

Improved code:
if (_hasInitialPosition && _popupPositionRequest?.Placement == PlacementMode.Pointer)
{
    reposition = false;
}

if (reposition)
{
    _needsUpdate = true;
    UpdatePosition();

    if (_popupPositionRequest != null && PlatformImpl != null)
    {
        _hasInitialPosition = true;
    }
}

The test expects `Position` to remain `(0,0)`, which is also the default value, so it passes even if the position was never set or was reset; it cannot detect the regression. Assert that the pointer location differs from the origin initially, then that the position is unchanged after the resize. Also, `host?.Height = 40` (null-conditional assignment) requires C# 14; use a non-null cast and a plain assignment.

Improved code:
var contentHost = Assert.IsAssignableFrom<ContentControl>(flyout.Popup.Host);
var initialPosition = platformImpl!.Position;

contentHost.Height = 40;
contentHost.InvalidateMeasure();
contentHost.Measure(Size.Infinity);
contentHost.Arrange(new Rect(default, contentHost.DesiredSize));

Assert.Equal(initialPosition, platformImpl.Position);

The only condition is `viewportEnd >= Bounds.Height/Width` (plus `!_hasReachedEnd`). It does not detect a scroll to the end. It is also true when:
- the panel is not inside a scroller, or its content fits, so the viewport equals the bounds;
- Bounds is still 0x0 before the first arrange, so `GreaterThanOrClose(0, 0)` or any positive viewport passes;
- Bounds is stale from a previous extent, e.g. after items were replaced or the viewport was resized.

In these cases realization is anchored on item `itemCount - 1`, positioned at `viewportEnd - estimate`, and earlier items are laid out backwards from an estimate. Small lists can then start at a non-zero or negative offset, and an initial layout may realize from the end instead of the start.

Suggestion: Only take this path for a genuine scroll-to-end. For example, require that the extent is larger than the viewport (`viewportEnd > viewportSize` / the viewport start is > 0), that Bounds is non-empty, and ideally that the request came from an offset change rather than any measure.

`?.ScrollToEnd()` silently does nothing if the template part isn't resolved, so the test fails with a confusing assertion instead of a clear null failure.

