The new "anchor at end" shortcut compares the viewport end against `Bounds`. `Bounds` holds the size from the previous arrange pass. Before the first arrange, `Bounds` is still zero, so any non-zero viewport start satisfies the check. The panel then jumps to the last item instead of estimating the correct anchor. Only apply this shortcut when the panel has a valid, non-zero arranged size.

Improved code:
var horizontal = Orientation == Orientation.Horizontal;
var viewportEnd = horizontal ? _viewport.Right : _viewport.Bottom;
var boundsU = horizontal ? Bounds.Width : Bounds.Height;

if (!_hasReachedEnd &&
    boundsU > 0 &&
    MathUtilities.GreaterThanOrClose(viewportEnd, boundsU))
{
    index = itemCount - 1;
    position = viewportEnd - EstimateElementSizeU();
    return;
}

This condition must stay identical to the one in `GetOrEstimateAnchorElementForViewport`. Otherwise the realization step may re-position an anchor that was not chosen by the end shortcut. For example, the last item may be picked through normal estimation while `Bounds` is zero. Apply the same non-zero bounds guard here so the two checks stay consistent.

Improved code:
var viewportEnd = horizontal ? _viewport.Right : _viewport.Bottom;
var boundsU = horizontal ? Bounds.Width : Bounds.Height;
var anchorAtEnd = !_hasReachedEnd && index == items.Count - 1 &&
    boundsU > 0 &&
    MathUtilities.GreaterThanOrClose(viewportEnd, boundsU);

This shortcut reads the raw `_viewport` field and the previous-layout `Bounds`, but neither may be valid yet. Before the first arrange, `Bounds` is 0, so any non-zero scroll offset (for example a restored or bound offset) makes the panel treat the viewport as "at end" and jump to the last item. If `_viewport` is still the invalid sentinel (infinite), `position` becomes infinite. Skip the shortcut unless `viewportEnd` is finite and the panel already has a non-empty size along the scroll axis.

Improved code:
var horizontal = Orientation == Orientation.Horizontal;
var viewportEnd = horizontal ? _viewport.Right : _viewport.Bottom;
var boundsU = horizontal ? Bounds.Width : Bounds.Height;

if (!_hasReachedEnd &&
    !double.IsInfinity(viewportEnd) && !double.IsNaN(viewportEnd) &&
    boundsU > 0 &&
    MathUtilities.GreaterThanOrClose(viewportEnd, boundsU))
{
    index = itemCount - 1;
    position = viewportEnd - EstimateElementSizeU();
    return;
}

`anchorAtEnd` repeats the same unguarded check. When `Bounds` is still 0 or `_viewport` is infinite, it re-anchors the last element to `viewportEnd - sizeU`. That produces wrong or infinite element positions and overwrites `viewport.anchorU`. Apply the same validity guards here so this matches the anchor-estimation logic.

Improved code:
var viewportEnd = horizontal ? _viewport.Right : _viewport.Bottom;
var boundsU = horizontal ? Bounds.Width : Bounds.Height;
var anchorAtEnd = !_hasReachedEnd && index == items.Count - 1 &&
    !double.IsInfinity(viewportEnd) && !double.IsNaN(viewportEnd) &&
    boundsU > 0 &&
    MathUtilities.GreaterThanOrClose(viewportEnd, boundsU);

When `Bounds` is still empty (for example, an offset was applied before the first arrange), `viewportEnd >= 0` is always true. This wrongly jumps the anchor to the last item. Require a non-zero panel extent before using the "at end" shortcut. Apply the same guard to `anchorAtEnd` in `RealizeElements` so both checks agree.

Improved code:
var boundsU = horizontal ? Bounds.Width : Bounds.Height;

if (!_hasReachedEnd &&
    boundsU > 0 &&
    MathUtilities.GreaterThanOrClose(viewportEnd, boundsU))
{
    index = itemCount - 1;
    position = viewportEnd - EstimateElementSizeU();
    return;
}

`anchorAtEnd` is recomputed from `index == items.Count - 1` and stale `Bounds`. It can therefore also override an anchor that was chosen legitimately by another path, such as the realized-elements or `ScrollIntoView` path. In addition, `u` can become negative when the last item is larger than `viewportEnd`. Clamp `u` to zero so earlier items are never positioned before the panel origin.

Improved code:
if (anchorAtEnd && index == viewport.anchorIndex)
{
    u = Math.Max(0, viewportEnd - sizeU);
    viewport.anchorU = u;
}

This PR removes the public `NonQueryExpression` type and the `protected virtual VisitNonQuery` method. That is source and binary breaking for relational providers that override or call them. Keep an overridable hook so providers can still customize non-query compilation, and call the break out explicitly in the PR (it also changes `Type` from `object` to `void`). If possible, keep the old members as obsolete shims.

Improved code:
protected override Expression VisitExtension(Expression extensionExpression)
    => extensionExpression switch
    {
        UpdateExpression updateExpression => VisitNonQuery(updateExpression.ApplyTags(_tags), CommandSource.ExecuteUpdate),
        DeleteExpression deleteExpression => VisitNonQuery(deleteExpression.ApplyTags(_tags), CommandSource.ExecuteDelete),
        _ => base.VisitExtension(extensionExpression)
    };

/// <summary>
///     Visits the given non-query expression (an <see cref="UpdateExpression" /> or <see cref="DeleteExpression" />), returning an
///     expression that when compiled, can execute the non-query operation against the database.
/// </summary>
/// <param name="nonQueryExpression">The expression to be compiled.</param>
/// <param name="commandSource">The source of the command.</param>
/// <returns>An expression which executes a non-query operation.</returns>
protected virtual Expression VisitNonQuery(Expression nonQueryExpression, CommandSource commandSource)
{
    var relationalCommandResolver = CreateRelationalCommandResolverExpression(nonQueryExpression);

    return Call(
        QueryCompilationContext.IsAsync ? NonQueryAsyncMethodInfo : NonQueryMethodInfo,
        Convert(QueryCompilationContext.QueryContextParameter, typeof(RelationalQueryContext)),
        relationalCommandResolver,
        Constant(_contextType),
        Constant(commandSource),
        Constant(_threadSafetyChecksEnabled));
}

