for (var i = start; i < start + size && i < items.Count; i++)

`customerPrefix` is concatenated directly into the SQL text. Input such as `x' OR 1=1; DROP TABLE orders; --` executes arbitrary SQL against the database.

Suggestion: cmd.CommandText = "SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders WHERE customer LIKE @p";
var p = cmd.CreateParameter(); p.ParameterName = "@p"; p.Value = EscapeLike(customerPrefix) + "%"; cmd.Parameters.Add(p);

CA2100 is correct here. `customerPrefix` is caller-controlled and is concatenated straight into the SQL text. Input like `' OR 1=1; DROP TABLE orders; --` runs arbitrary SQL against the database. `FindByCustomerAsync` in the same codebase already uses parameters.

Suggestion: cmd.CommandText = "SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders WHERE customer LIKE @prefix";
var p = cmd.CreateParameter();
p.ParameterName = "@prefix";
